-- 5. Role of K_L (64 bits -> two 16-element permutations) ---
   K_L can address at most 2^64 table pairs, but there are
   (16!)^2 = 2^88.5 possible pairs,
   so the labyrinth space is capped by the key, not by the tables
   200,000 random K_L -> 200,000 distinct table pairs (0 collisions, birthday expectation 1.08e-09)
   [PASS] distinct K_L give distinct labyrinths   0 collisions
   
   [PASS] fixed K_L, varying K_0..K_3 still gives distinct schedules   0 collisions in 2000
   [PASS] fixed K_0..K_3, varying K_L still gives distinct schedules   0 collisions in 2000
   [PASS] a single K_L bit flips ~50% of all subkey bits   49.94%

-- 6. Recovering the schedule state from known subkeys -------
   k_i = S_{i mod 4} + RC_i, so a known k_i exposes exactly ONE
   48-bit state word -- and only as it stood at step i.
   [PASS] each k_i exposes exactly one state word of step i
   [PASS] four consecutive subkeys do NOT compose into a state snapshot   only 1/4 words coincide with the real step-3 state
   This is a real strength of the design: an attacker who learns
   several subkeys still never sees the 192-bit state at one instant.
   [PASS] a FULL state snapshot plus K_L does predict every later subkey   so the schedule protects the state, not the forward direction
   [NOTE] Summary: k_i leaks one word per step, never a full snapshot. Going backwards additionally needs K_L and branches through non-injective Lab. No shortcut below 2^256 was found.

